# Java/Kotlin + Spring security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic security rules to Spring Boot
3.x-specific, OWASP-relevant risks. Broadly scoped to all Java/Kotlin
sources for data-access and secrets guidance below, with extra emphasis
on controller/security-config files for the HTTP-facing rules.

## Spring Security configuration

- Configure security with a `SecurityFilterChain` `@Bean` returned from a
  `@Configuration` class (`http.build()` on an `HttpSecurity` you have
  customized) — this is the current Spring Boot 3.x idiom. Do not extend
  `WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter`; it was removed in Spring Security 6 /
  Spring Boot 3 and any code still referencing it is targeting an old
  major version.
- Scope authorization rules explicitly (`authorizeHttpRequests` with
  path/role matchers) rather than leaving a catch-all `permitAll()` that
  was only meant for one endpoint during development.

## CSRF

- A stateless, token-authenticated API (bearer JWT/OAuth2, no session
  cookie) legitimately disables CSRF protection (`csrf(AbstractHttpConfigurer::disable)`)
  because CSRF exploits ambient cookie auth, which does not apply here —
  state this reasoning explicitly when disabling it.
- A session-cookie-authenticated endpoint (browser form login, `JSESSIONID`)
  must keep CSRF protection enabled; disabling it there reopens the exact
  attack the stateless-API exception does not apply to.

## SQL and data access

- Use Spring Data JPA derived queries or parameterized `@Query`
  (JPQL/native, with named or positional bind parameters), never
  string-concatenated or `String.format`-built SQL/JPQL with any
  user-influenced value — parameterization is the injection defense, not
  an input-validation substitute for it.
- The same applies to `JdbcTemplate`/`NamedParameterJdbcTemplate`: use its
  parameter-binding API, never interpolate a value into the SQL string.

## Input validation as the DTO boundary defense

- Validate untrusted input at the DTO boundary with `jakarta.validation`
  annotations plus `@Valid`/`@Validated` on the controller method — this
  is the primary injection/malformed-input defense point for request
  data, on top of (not instead of) parameterized queries downstream.

## Secrets and configuration

- Never hard-code a credential, API key, or signing secret in source or
  in `application.yml`/`application.properties` committed to the repo —
  load it from environment variables, a config server, or the secret
  store the project already uses (Spring's externalized configuration
  supports both cleanly).
- Do not log a request/response body that may carry credentials, tokens,
  or PII at `INFO`/`DEBUG` level without redaction.

## Dependency hygiene

- Run the project's configured dependency-vulnerability check (OWASP
  Dependency-Check plugin, `./gradlew dependencyCheckAnalyze`, or
  whatever the build already wires up) when introducing or bumping a
  dependency, rather than skipping it because "it's just a minor bump."
